; redox: electron transfer between chemical species.
; oxidation: loss of electrons (OIL — Oxidation Is Loss).
; reduction: gain of electrons (RIG — Reduction Is Gain).
; they always occur together — one species loses what another gains.
; 
; oxidation state: formal charge if all bonds were ionic.
; oxidation: oxidation state increases (more positive).
; reduction: oxidation state decreases (more negative).
; 
; the oxidising agent: accepts electrons (gets reduced).
; the reducing agent: donates electrons (gets oxidised).
; 
; everyday examples:
; rusting: 4Fe + 3O2 → 2Fe2O3  (Fe oxidised 0→+3, O reduced 0→-2)
; fire: C + O2 → CO2  (C oxidised 0→+4)
; battery: chemical → electrical energy via controlled redox
; photosynthesis: CO2 reduced to glucose (light drives the reaction)
; respiration: glucose oxidised to CO2 (energy released as ATP)
; bleaching: oxidising agent destroys colour by oxidising chromophores
; 
; electrochemistry: redox reactions in solution.
; standard electrode potential E°: measure of tendency to be reduced.
; Nernst equation: E = E° - (RT/nF)ln(Q).

